$DIR/main.p:11:13: cannot borrow an error returned by (root).Unwrap: the wrapping error may not keep it alive
    Unwrap returns a counted error that is not held by the wrapping error. A
    pointer target of errors.As only borrows that child. The child is released
    when the search leaves it, so keeping the wrapping error alive does not keep
    the target alive.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Use a counted target matching the child's dynamic type (shared[T] or
    synced[T]), or store the child in a field of the wrapping error
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3003" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:14:59: cannot pass x.Unwrap() to as, which keeps it after returning
    x.Unwrap() points into the object of a temporary error in as's frame. The
    error made at main.p:14 is a temporary: nothing keeps it, so its object is
    dropped at the end of the statement, and a view of the object may not
    outlive that statement. as stores it in *target (main.p:13), so x.Unwrap()
    would outlive as's frame.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Change as to take a value or an owner, which it can keep: an owned[T] from
    new, a shared[T], or a library owner such as a vec.Vec.
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3003" for a longer explanation)
